# Request path for api.campus.test

## Resolution

$ dig +short api.campus.test A
$ dig +trace api.campus.test

The name must resolve to the load balancer address, not to a host being retired.

## TCP and TLS handshake

$ curl -v --max-time 5 https://api.campus.test/health
$ openssl s_client -connect api.campus.test:443 -servername api.campus.test

Check the certificate subject and expiry in the s_client output. A name mismatch or an expired certificate fails the connection before any request reaches the proxy.

## Proxy block

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name api.campus.test;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://api_upstream;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_connect_timeout 3s;
        proxy_read_timeout 10s;
    }

    location = /health {
        proxy_pass http://api_upstream/health;
        proxy_connect_timeout 1s;
        proxy_read_timeout 2s;
    }
}

The upstream reads the client address from X-Forwarded-For and the scheme from X-Forwarded-Proto. Without both headers, every request looks like it came from the proxy over plain HTTP, so logs and redirects are wrong.

## Health check

$ curl -fsS --max-time 2 https://api.campus.test/health

The probe calls /health, which checks the database, not just the process. A running worker with an unreachable database must fail the probe.
